==English==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:clip|merchandise<id:noun>|tree=1|text=+}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/mɝt͡ʃ/|a=GenAm}}
* {{IPA|en|/mɜːtʃ/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-merch.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)tʃ|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} goods which are or were offered or intended for sale.
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=2007 February 15|author=Eric Wilson|title=Babes in Label Land| url=http://www.nytimes.com/2007/02/15/fashion/shows/15ROW.html| passage=His playful point of view on youthful American sportswear, expressed in boxy mohair jackets with graphic resin bubble buttons and tomboy T-shirt dresses in superfine chiffon as bright as tangerine sorbet, is backed up with what stores would describe as the '''merch''': easy scoop-neck cashmere sweaters in a mix of colors, lightweight T-shirts and sheared mink Army caps.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230415111153/https://www.nytimes.com/2007/02/15/fashion/shows/15ROW.html|archivedate=15 April 2023}}
## {{lb|en|especially|in|_|entertainment|sports|marketing}} goods connected with an entity whose primary activity is not the production or sale of such goods, such as a team, band, work of fiction, etc.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Jesse Cannon; Todd Thomas|title=Get More Fans: The DIY Guide to the New Music Business
|passage=Many musicians make '''merch''' you can only get if you join the fanclub.}}
##* {{RQ:Hollywood Reporter| title=Still Fired Up? Hillary Clinton '''Merch''' Marked Down to Move| date=11/15/2016| author=Sam Reed| passage=With almost two months until Inauguration Day, fans who were "With Her" (or are still "With Her") can grab Clinton '''merch''' — now at deeply marked-down prices.}}
##* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=October 19. 2019| title='OK Boomer' Marks the End of Friendly Generational Relations| passage=The '''merch''' is proof of how much the sentiment resonates with people.}}

